\newlabel{fig: rotated balls}{{1}{2}{Visualising Lang and Tan's $5p$ claim. The blue horoballs are project to Haas's cusp region and the red ones their image under $z \mapsto (\frac 15(3 - 4i))^2\cdot z$. Note that there are 2 purple discs tangent at $2/1$ and $1/2$. \relax }{figure.caption.1}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces  Blue discs are horoballs tangent to the unit circle to the images of rationals under the Cayley transformation. The projection to the modular torus is exactly Haas's cusp region - disjoint from every simple geodesic but meets evern non simple closed geodesic. If the tangency point $p/q$ is \textit  {visible from $O$}, that is the radius from $0$ to $p/q$ crosses just one blue disc, then $p^2 + q^2$ is a Markoff number.\relax }}{3}{figure.caption.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig: Ford circles}{{2}{3}{Blue discs are horoballs tangent to the unit circle to the images of rationals under the Cayley transformation. The projection to the modular torus is exactly Haas's cusp region - disjoint from every simple geodesic but meets evern non simple closed geodesic. If the tangency point $p/q$ is \textit {visible from $O$}, that is the radius from $0$ to $p/q$ crosses just one blue disc, then $p^2 + q^2$ is a Markoff number.\relax }{figure.caption.2}{}}
